I read an article that explained the development history of React. I have always firmly believed that the emergence of any technical solution is to solve problems in a better way.
## React component development history
React components can basically be divided into three stages:
-createClassComponents
- Class Components
-Function Components
### createClassComponents
Example:
```javascript
const Index = React.createClass({
  getDefaultProps() {
    return {};
  },
  getInitialState() {
    return {
      name: "robin",
    };
  },
  render() {
    return <div></div>;
  },
});
```
This was an early way of creating components, I guess at the time JavaScript didn't have a built-in class system. That all changed when ES6 arrived.
### Class Components
Example:
```javascript
class Index extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = {
      name: "robin",
    };
  }

  render() {
    return <div></div>;
  }
}
```
The way to initialize properties and states has changed accordingly. You may have questions here. If createClass is used well, why should you use Class inheritance to write components? I think it is to cater to the development of ES.
As the complexity of the project increases, is it a bit heavy if all components in the project are developed using classes? This time it comes.
### Function Components
Example:
```javascript
const Button = (props) => {
  return <div></div>;
};
```
I just want to encapsulate a public component and use it.
