This kind of composite component requires the intervention of state, but there is no need to use class components, so React Hooks were born.
## Introduction to React Hooks
- useState()
- useContext()
- useReducer()
- useEffect()
### useState
If operation status is required, the code is as follows:
```javascript
import React, { useState } from "react";

export default function TextInput() {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");

  return <input value={name} onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} />;
}
```
### useContext
If you need to share state, the code is as follows:
```javascript
const AppContext = React.createContext({});

const Button1 = () => {
  const { name } = useContext(AppContext);
  return <div>{name}</div>;
};
const Button2 = () => {
  const { name, age } = useContext(AppContext);

  return (
    <div>
      {name}
      {age}
    </div>
  );
};

const Button3 = () => {
  const [name, setName] = useState("robin111");
  return (
    <AppContext.Provider
      value={{
        name: name,
        age: 29,
      }}
    >
      <Button1 />
      <Button2 />
      <button onClick={() => setName("test")}>211</button>
    </AppContext.Provider>
  );
};
```
When shared state components are nested, the current context value is determined by the value prop of the <AppContext.Provider> closest to the current component in the upper component.
### useReducer
Redux provides a state management solution, where you can also use useReducer.
```javascript
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialArg, init);
```
Drawing on teacher Ruan Yifeng’s counter example:
```javascript
const myReducer = (state, action) => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "countUp":
      return {
        ...state,
        count: state.count + 1,
      };
    default:
      return state;
  }
};

function App() {
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(myReducer, { count: 0 });
  return (
    <div className="App">
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: "countUp" })}>+1</button>
      <p>Count: {state.count}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```
### useEffect
Official description: Effect Hook allows you to perform side-effect operations in function components.
```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from "react";

function Example() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = `You clicked ${count} times`;
  });

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click me</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```
This code is provided by the official and is concise and clear.
The official introduction to Effect is very detailed, and I highly recommend everyone to read it in detail. Detailed explanation of Chinese community
